From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] mountmgr: USB sticks and memory cards are removable disks

Purpose: a USB stick that Linux mounts is a Removable Disk in XP's My
Computer, with one drive letter.
Windows behavior: a stick or the card in a reader is DRIVE_REMOVABLE.
IOCTL_STORAGE_QUERY_PROPERTY says that its media is removable, its bus (USB
or SD), vendor, product and serial number. A disk has no letter of its own
beside its partitions.
Wine before: every block device whose UDisks2 drive is removable got a
letter, of unknown type unless the drive listed optical or floppy media: a
partitioned stick got one for the whole disk and one for each partition (the
disk's and a swap or empty partition's could not be opened). Once mounted it
was DRIVE_FIXED, a "Local Disk" with a hard disk's icon, as mountmgr knew no
type and ntdll marks only CD and floppy file systems removable. UDisks2's
serial number was not passed on to drive letters, and the storage descriptor
had no vendor or product and said fixed media on a SCSI bus.
Change: of a removable drive that is neither optical nor floppy, only a block
device with a file system to mount (UDisks2's Filesystem interface, a size,
neither HintIgnore nor HintSystem) is a drive, of type DEVICE_HARDDISK, which
is reported as DRIVE_REMOVABLE; one that loses its file system (a card taken
out of its reader) loses its letter. The disk keeps UDisks2's drive ID as its serial
number, and its vendor, model and bus for the storage descriptor. Where the
device cannot be read, FAT and exFAT are reported as FAT32, and their ID
(IdUUID) is the volume serial number, which was 0. ntddstor.h gets the bus
types from BusTypeiScsi to BusTypeMmc.
Evidence: tests/hotplug-probe.c in the XP session on the bench with
tools/bench/udisks-mock.py: a partitioned stick, one without a partition
table and one with two partitions. On the EeePC a Debian installer stick: its
whole disk has IdUsage "filesystem" (iso9660) beside the partition table and
no Filesystem interface, as UDisks2 offers only the partition to mount.
Scope: all programs; only drives UDisks2 reports as removable.
